      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "$\\frac{3+2x}{2x+3}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we notice that $2x+3=3+2x.$ So we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3+2x}{2x+3}=\\frac{3+2x}{3+2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3+2x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3+2x}{3+2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(3+2x)}}{\\cancel{(3+2x)}} & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $1.$",
